        "content": "Documents give different answers about the same thing. Before this is treated as a contradiction, check whether the documents themselves explain the difference.\n\nQuestion: Where does Johannes Moreelse work?\n\nAnswer A: Rome\n  about: Johannes Moreelse\n  sense: not stated\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"However, it was his fascination with the Italian Renaissance that drove him to seek inspiration in the very heart of the art world – Rome.\" [document 1]\n\nAnswer B: a young Dutch painter\n  about: Johannes Moreelse\n  sense: not stated\n  as of: 1625\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"In a small, yet bustling atelier nestled between the grand boulevards and winding alleys of the Latin Quarter, a young Dutch painter named Johannes Moreelse poured his heart and soul into his craft.\" [document 2]\n\nDocuments:\nDocument 1: \"The Eternal City's Brush with Fate: Johannes Moreelse's Roman Sojourn\"\n\nIn the early 17th century, the Eternal City of Rome was abuzz with artistic innovation, attracting talents from across Europe. Amidst this creative fervor, a young Dutch painter named Johannes Moreelse made his way to the Italian capital, leaving an indelible mark on the city's art scene. This narrative delves into the life and works of Moreelse, weaving together historical evidence to support the claim that he indeed worked in Rome. Born around 1603 in Utrecht, Netherlands, Moreelse was trained in the studio of the renowned painter, Abraham Bloemaert. The young artist's early works showcased a mastery of chiaroscuro, a hallmark of the Dutch Baroque style. However, it was his fascination with the Italian Renaissance that drove him to seek inspiration in the very heart of the art world – Rome. In 1627, Moreelse arrived in Rome, where he was drawn into the orbit of the city's vibrant artistic community. The Eternal City was a hotbed of creative activity, with masters like Caravaggio and Bernini pushing the boundaries of art. Moreelse's exposure to this rich cultural landscape had a profound impact on his style, as he began to incorporate the dramatic lighting and intense emotionalism characteristic of Roman Baroque art. One of the most compelling pieces of evidence supporting Moreelse's Roman sojourn is his \"Portrait of a Young Man\" (c. 1629), now housed in the Galleria Borghese. This exquisite work showcases the artist's ability to capture the subtleties of human emotion, a trait he likely developed during his time in Rome. The subject's introspective gaze, set against a backdrop of deep shadows, is reminiscent of Caravaggio's innovative use of light and darkness. Moreelse's Roman period also saw him experiment with mythological themes, as evident in his \"Bacchus and Ariadne\" (c. 1630). This painting, now in the collection of the Musée des Beaux-Arts in Lyon, demonstrates the artist's growing confidence in his use of color and composition. The work's dynamic energy, coupled with its classical subject matter, suggests that Moreelse was deeply influenced by the city's rich cultural heritage. Further evidence of Moreelse's Roman connection can be found in his association with the city's prominent art patrons.\nDocument 2: As the Seine River flowed gently through the heart of Paris, its tranquil waters reflected the vibrant hues of the city's artistic soul. In a small, yet bustling atelier nestled between the grand boulevards and winding alleys of the Latin Quarter, a young Dutch painter named Johannes Moreelse poured his heart and soul into his craft. The year was 1625, and the city was alive with the whispers of revolution, as the avant-garde minds of the era converged upon the City of Light. Moreelse, with his wild shock of curly brown hair and piercing green eyes, was a fixture in the Parisian art scene. His days were filled with the soft scratch of his brushes against canvas, as he brought to life the dramatic, sweeping landscapes and intimate portraits that would soon earn him acclaim across the continent. His nights, however, were reserved for the city's vibrant salons, where he would engage in lively debates with the likes of René Descartes and Pierre Corneille, exchanging ideas on the very nature of art and reality. One fateful evening, as the candles flickered and the wine flowed, Moreelse found himself entranced by the enigmatic Madame de Rambouillet, a patron of the arts and a fierce defender of the city's creative spirit. Her eyes, like polished onyx, sparkled with a fire that seemed to fuel Moreelse's own creative passion, as she commissioned him to create a series of paintings that would capture the essence of Paris itself. And so, Moreelse set out to immortalize the city's beauty, from the majestic grandeur of the Louvre to the whimsical charm of the Pont des Arts. As he wandered the city's streets, his sketchbook and pencils in hand, he felt the very fabric of Paris weaving itself into the tapestry of his art. The city, it seemed, had become his muse, his confidante, and his home. In the years that followed, Moreelse's paintings would become synonymous with the Parisian school of art, a testament to the city's power to inspire and transform those who called it home.\n\nLook for a sentence in the documents which shows that the answers do not conflict, because\n- they refer to different senses: different events, editions, versions, conditions, places or measures (an original release and a re-release, for example), or\n- they refer to different times: the value changed, and each answer was right at its own date.\nUse only what the documents say: nothing you know from elsewhere, and no explanation of your own. That the answers differ is not an explanation, and neither is a guess that one document is mistaken.\n\nReply with JSON only. If a sentence explains the difference:\n{\"explained\": true, \"by\": \"sense\", \"document\": 1, \"quote\": \"\", \"labels\": {\"A\": \"\", \"B\": \"\"}}\n\"by